  <h1 class="header-title" style="margin-top: 30px;">Publication 925</h1>
  <div style="font-size: 10pt; margin-bottom: 30px;">Cat. No. <span data-field="catalog_number">{{ catalog_number }}</span></div>

  <h1 class="header-title" style="text-align: center;">Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules</h1>

  <div style="text-align: center; margin-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 16pt; font-weight: normal;">For use in preparing</h2>
    <h1 style="font-size: 24pt; font-weight: normal;">2012 Returns</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="two-column" style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Future Developments</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>For the latest information about developments related to Publication 925, such as legislation enacted after it was published, go to <a href="http://www.irs.gov/pub/pub925">www.irs.gov/pub/pub925</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Reminders</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p><strong>At-risk amounts.</strong> The following rules apply to amounts borrowed after May 3, 2004.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>You must file Form 6198, At-Risk Limits, if you are engaged in an activity in-cluded in (6) under <a href="#">Activities Covered by the At-Risk Rules</a> and you have borrowed amounts excluded under <a href="#">Certain borrowed amounts</a> in this publication.</li>
          <li>You may be considered at risk for certain amounts described in <a href="#">Certain borrowed amounts excluded under At-Risk Amounts</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Photographs of missing children.</strong> The Internal Revenue Service is a proud partner with the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children. Photographs of missing children selected by the Center may appear in this publication on pages that would otherwise be blank. You can help bring these children home by looking at the</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column">
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Introduction</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>This publication discusses two sets of rules that may limit the amount of your deductible loss from a trade or business, rental, or other in-come-producing activity. The passive activity rules discuss the passive activity rules. The second part discusses the at-risk rules. The at-risk rules are a set of rules that says from any activity, you must apply the at-risk rules before the passive activity rules.</p>
        <p><strong>Comments and suggestions.</strong> We welcome your comments about this publication and your suggestions for future editions. Please write to the following address:</p>
        <p>Internal Revenue Service</p>
        <p>Individual and Specialty Forms and Publications Branch</p>
        <p>SE-CARF-MP-T1</p>
        <p>111 Constitution Ave., NW, IR-6526</p>
        <p>Washington, DC 20224</p>
        <p>We respond to many letters by telephone. Therefore, it would be helpful if you would in-clude your phone number, including the area code, in your correspondence.</p>
        <p>Please put "Publication 925" on the subject line. You can also send us comments from <a href="#">www.irs.gov</a> or by mail to the address above. For more infor-mation, see <a href="#">IRS.gov</a>.</p>
        <p>We cannot respond individually to each comment received, we do appreciate your feedback and will consider your comments as we prepare future editions.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Ordering forms and publications.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Visit <a href="#">www.irs.gov/forms/publications</a> to download forms and publications, call 1-800-897-2995, or write to the address below to receive a response within an appropriate timeframe if your request is received.</p>
          <p>Internal Revenue Service</p>
          <p>1201 N. Mississippi Midway</p>
          <p>Bloomington, IL 61705-6613</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Tax questions.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>If you have a tax question, check the information available on <a href="#">IRS.gov</a> or call 1-800-129-8300. We cannot answer tax questions sent to other than the above ad-dresses.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Useful items.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>You may want to see:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><span data-field="publication_number">{{ publication_number }}</span> Residential Rental Property (Including Rental of Vacation Homes)</li>
            <li><span data-field="publication_number">{{ publication_number }}</span> Partnerships</li>
            <li>Form (and Instructions) <span data-field="form_number">{{ form_number }}</span> Investment Interest Expense Deduction</li>
            <li><span data-field="form_number">{{ form_number }}</span> At-Risk Limitations</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Passive Activity Limits</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p><strong>Passive activity.</strong> An activity is passive if it does not meet the definition of an active activity. An activity is active if it is carried on by you in a trade or business, or if it is a rental activity.</p>
        <p><strong>Who Must Use These Rules?</strong></p>
        <p>The passive activity rules apply to:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Individuals.</li>
          <li>Estates.</li>
          <li>Trusts (other than grantor trusts).</li>
          <li>Personal service corporations, and</li>
          <li>Closely held corporations.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Even though the rules do not apply to gran-tor trusts, partnerships, and S corporations di-rectly, they do apply to the owners of these entit-ies.</p>
        <p>For information about personal service cor-porations, see Form 8852 for information about how to apply the passive activity rules to these corporations, see Form 8810 and its instructions.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Loss from an activity.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>The term "loss" from an activity means:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>The amount by which the passive activity deductions (defined below) exceed the passive activity income (defined below) for the tax year for the tax year;</li>
            <li>Any part of such amount that is allowed under the <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>If your passive activity gross income from significant participation passive activities (de-fined later) for the tax year is more than your passive activity deductions, you may deduct the excess loss for such taxable year. See <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
          <p>If your passive activity gross income from significant participation passive activities exceeds under <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>, you may be considered at risk for certain amounts described in <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Passive Activity Loss</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Generally, the passive activity loss for the tax year is limited to the amount of your passive activity income, whether some or all of your pas-sive activity loss may be allowed. See <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
          <div class="sub-heading">Definition of passive activity.</div>
          <div class="content-text">
            <p>Generally, your passive activity loss for the tax year is limited to the amount of your passive activity income. An activity is passive if it is not an active activity. An activity is active if it is carried on by you in a trade or business, or if it is a rental activity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-heading">Identification of Disallowed Passive Activity Deductions</div>
          <div class="content-text">
            <p>If all or a part of your passive activity loss is dis-allowed for the tax year, you may need to allo-cate the disallowed passive activity loss among</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column">
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Activities That Are Not Passive Activities</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>The following are not passive activities:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Trade or business activities in which you materially participate for the tax year.</li>
          <li>Rental activities in which you materially participate for the tax year.</li>
          <li>Activities in which you are a real estate professional.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Real estate professional.</strong></p>
        <p>You can treat yourself as a real estate professional if you meet all of the following requirements:</p>
        <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>You must materially participate in real estate activities for more than seven months of the tax year.</li>
          <li>You must participate in real estate activities for more than 750 hours during the tax year.</li>
          <li>You must not have more than 50% of your total personal services during the tax year devoted to non-real estate activities.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If you qualify as a real estate professional, you may treat all your rental activities as nonpassive.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Material Participation</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Material participation is a test that determines whether you are materially participating in an activity. You may materially participate in an activity if you meet any of the following tests:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You participate in the activity for substantially all the hours during the tax year.</li>
            <li>Your participation in the activity for the tax year is more than 500 hours.</li>
            <li>Your participation in the activity for the tax year is more than 100 hours and you participated in the activity for more than 3 of the five consecutive tax years ending with the tax year.</li>
            <li>Your participation in the activity for the tax year is more than 100 hours and you participated in the activity for more than 1 of the five consecutive tax years ending with the tax year.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You may also materially participate in an activity if you are a partner in a partnership and your participation in the activity is more than 500 hours during the tax year.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Passive Activity Income and Deductions</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>In figuring your net income or loss from a pas-sive activity, take into account only passive ac-tivity income and passive activity deductions.</p>
          <p>Self-entered interest. Certain self-charged interest, certain self-loan interest, and certain self-borrowed interest are passive activity deductions if the loan proceeds are used in a passive activity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Trade or Business Activities</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>A trade or business activity is an activity that:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Involves the conduct of a trade or business.</li>
          <li>Involves the conduct of a trade or business for profit.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The term "trade or business" includes:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Any activity that is conducted with the intent of making a profit.</li>
          <li>Any activity that is conducted in a regular and continuous manner.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A trade or business activity does not include a rental activity or the holding of property for investment.</p>
        <p>You generally report trade or business ac-tivities on Schedule C, E, F, or P in Part II of your tax return.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Rental Activities</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>A rental activity is a passive activity even if you materially participate in that activity. Unless you qualify as a real estate professional, you are treated as materially participating in the rental activity if you meet any of the following tests:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You rented the property for more than 15 days during the tax year.</li>
            <li>You rented the property for more than 100 days during the tax year.</li>
            <li>You rented the property for more than 100 days during the tax year and you had the property available for rent for more than 100 days during the tax year.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>If you qualify as a real estate professional, you may treat all your rental activities as nonpassive.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Exceptions.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Exceptions. Your activity is not a rental activity if:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>You are engaged in the activity for the purpose of holding property for investment.</li>
            <li>You are engaged in the activity for the purpose of holding property for investment.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The average period of customer use of the property is more than 15 days. You are engaged in the activity for the purpose of holding property for investment. The average period of customer use of the property is more than 15 days. The average period of customer use of the property is more than 15 days.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-heading">Passive Activity Income</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Passive activity income includes all income from passive activities and generally cannot be offset by losses from nonpassive activities.</p>
          <p>Passive activity income does not include the following:</p>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>Income from a not a passive activity.</li>
            <li>Income from a not a passive activity.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Portfolio income. This includes interest, dividends, annuities, and royalties, and any other income from property held for investment. Portfolio income is not passive activity income.</p>
          <p>Passive activity income includes all income from passive activities, except for portfolio income.</p>
          <p>Passive activity income includes all income from passive activities, except for portfolio income. For more information on self-charged interest, see <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column">
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Passive Activity Deductions</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>Passive activity deductions generally include:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Deductions for expenses other than interest and taxes.</li>
          <li>Deductions for depreciation and amortization.</li>
          <li>Deductions for losses from property.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Passive activity deductions generally do not include the following:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Deductions for other than interest and taxes.</li>
          <li>Deductions for depreciation and amortization.</li>
          <li>Deductions for losses from property.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For more information on the deduction for expenses, see <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Coordination with other limitations on deductions</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>The passive activity loss limitation is subject to coordination with other limitations on deductions, such as the at-risk limitation and the basis limitation.</p>
        <p>For more information about these limitations, see <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span> and <span data-field="section_reference">{{ section_reference }}</span>.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Proration of deductions disallowed un-der the passive activity loss limitation.</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>If you have a passive activity loss that exceeds your passive activity income, you may be required to prorate your deductions. The amount of the loss is limited to the amount of your passive activity income. The amount of the loss is limited to the amount of your passive activity income. The amount of the loss is limited to the amount of your passive activity income.</p>
          <p>The amount of the loss is limited to the amount of your passive activity income. The amount of the loss is limited to the amount of your passive activity income.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="two-column">
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Grouping Your Activities</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>Grouping is important for a number of reasons. First, it allows you to treat certain activities as a single activity. Second, it allows you to treat certain activities as a single activity for the purpose of the passive activity loss limitation.</p>
        <p>Grouping can also be important in determin-ing whether you meet the 10% ownership re-quirement for a real estate professional.</p>
        <div class="sub-heading">Appropriate Economic Units</div>
        <div class="content-text">
          <p>Generally, to determine if activities form an ap-propriate economic unit, you must consider all of the following factors:</p>
          <ol style="padding-left: 20px;">
            <li>The similarities and differences in the types of trades or businesses.</li>
            <li>The extent of common control.</li>
            <li>The extent of common ownership.</li>
            <li>The geographical location, and</li>
            <li>The interdependence between or among the activities.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>A. Buy or sell goods between or among themselves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-heading">Publication 925 (2012)</div>
      <div class="content-text">
        <p>Page <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
