<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Worksheet E. Worksheet E, Equity-Financed Lending Activities</div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you have gross income from an equity-financed lending activity, the lesser of the net passive income or the equity-financed interest income is nonpassive income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For more information, see Temporary Regulations Section 1.469-2T(i)(4).</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Worksheet F. Worksheet F, Rental of Property to a Development Activity</div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Net income from this type of activity will be treated as nonpassive income if all of the following apply:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 10pt; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>You recognize gain from the sale, exchange, or other disposition of the rental property during the tax year.</li>
        <li>You started to rent the property less than <span data-field="ws_f.time_limit">{{ ws_f.time_limit }}</span> prior to the tax year.</li>
        <li>You materially participated or significantly participated in the rental activity for the entire tax year.</li>
        <li>The property is owned by an entity that is a partnership or S corporation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-title">Worksheet G. Worksheet G, Licensing of Intangible Property by Pass-Through Entities</div>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Net royalty income from intangible property is treated as nonpassive royalty income if the entity that owns the intangible property is a pass-through entity.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This applies if you acquired your intangible property in a pass-through entity.</p>
  <div style="text-align: right; margin-top: 50px;">
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Publication 925 (2012)</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Page <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span></p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Part I</div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For tax years beginning after January 24, 2010, the following disclosure requirements group for passive activity losses. You must disclose the following to your tax return:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 10pt; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Whether you have passive activity losses.</li>
        <li>Whether you have passive activity losses that are attributable to the part of the activity disposed of.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you dispose of sub-stantially all of an activity during your tax year, you may be able to treat the disposed activity as nonpassive. However, you can only do this if you can show reasonable certainty.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Dispositions</div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you dispose of substantially all of an activity during your tax year, you may be able to treat the disposed activity as nonpassive. However, you can only do this if you can show reasonable certainty.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you have a capital loss on the disposition of an interest in a passive activity, you may be able to deduct the loss to the extent of your passive activity losses.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-title">Comprehensive Example</div>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The following example shows how to report your passive activity losses. In addition to Form 1040, Charles and Lily use Form 8582 (to figure out their passive activity losses) and Worksheet 1 (to figure out their passive activity loss limits).</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Charles and Lily use Form 8582 (to figure out their passive activity losses) and Worksheet 1 (to figure out their passive activity loss limits).</p>

  <div class="section-title">General Information</div>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Charles and Lily are married, file jointly, and have income from a rental activity and a business activity. They are at risk for their investment in the rental activity and the business activity. They did not materially participate in the rental activity or the business activity.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Charles and Lily use Form 8582 (to figure out their passive activity losses) and Worksheet 1 (to figure out their passive activity loss limits).</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">They use the passive activity loss limits to determine whether they can deduct any passive activity losses.</p>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt;">They use the passive activity loss limits to determine whether they can deduct any passive activity losses.</p>

  <div style="text-align: right; margin-top: 50px;">
