<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 16pt;">at-risk rules under the equipment leasing</h2>
  <p>Controlled group of corporations. A con-</p>
  <p>trolled group of corporations is subject to spe-</p>
  <p>cial rules for the equipment leasing exclusion. See section 465(c) of the Internal Revenue Code.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Special exception for qualified corpora-</h3>
  <p>tions. A qualified corporation may elect to be at-</p>
  <p>the at-risk limits for any qualifying business car-</p>
  <p>ried on by the corporation. Each qualifying busi-</p>
  <p>ness must meet certain requirements.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Qualified corporation.</h3>
  <p>A qualified corpo-</p>
  <p>ration is a closely held corporation. Defined</p>
  <p>earlier, that is not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A personal holding company, or</li>
    <li>A non-service corporation (defined in section 268A) of the Internal Revenue Code, due to being substituted by 5% or less for 10%.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Qualifying business.</h3>
  <p>A qualifying busi-</p>
  <p>ness is any active business if all of the following apply:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>During the entire 12-month period ended on the last day of the tax year, the corpo-</p>
    <p>ration must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Operate a sole-purpose wellness shop exer-</p>
      <li>cises in the active management of the business, and</li>
      <li>Have three full-time nonowner employees whose services are devoted to the business. A nonowner em-</p>
      <li>ployee is an employee who does not own more than 5% of the value in out-</p>
      <li>standing stock of the corporation at any time during the tax year. (The rules for constructive ownership of stock as set forth in section 318 of the Internal Revenue Code apply. However, in ap-</p>
      <li>plying these rules, an owner of 5% or less of the value of the corporation's stock is con-</p>
      <li>sidered to own a proportionate share of any stock owned by the corpora-</p>
      <li>tion.</li>
    </ul>
    <li>Deductions due to the business that are allowable to the corporation as business expenses and as contributions to certain retirement plans must be more than 65% of the income from the gross business.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The business is not an excluded business. Examples of excluded businesses include, an equipment leasing, as defined earlier, an</p>
  <p>exception for equipment leasing by a corporation, or a bank.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Separation of Activities</h3>
  <p>Generally, you treat your activity involving each film or video tape, item of leased section 1245 property, farm, oil and gas property, or</p>
  <p>geothermal property as a separate activity. In</p>
  <p>addition, there must not be a part of a trade or business treated as a separate activi-</p>
  <p>ty.</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 16pt;">Aggregation of Activities</h2>
  <p>Activities described in (6) under Activities Cov-</p>
  <p>erage are aggregated if the trade or busi-</p>
  <p>ness or trade or business is owned as one ac-</p>
  <p>tivity.</p>
  <p>This activity typically participates in the manage-</p>
  <p>ment of the trade or business, or</p>
  <p>the trade or business is carried on by a partnership or corporation and 65% or more of the income for the taxable year is attri-</p>
  <p>bable to persons who actively participate in the management of the trade or business.</p>
  <p>Similar rules apply to activities described in (1) under Activities Coverage.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Active participation.</h3>
  <p>Active participation de-</p>
  <p>fines all the persons and circumstances fac-</p>
  <p>tors that indicate active participation including making decisions involving the operation or</p>
  <p>management of the trade or business, or</p>
  <p>the activity, and hiring and discharging</p>
  <p>employees. Factors that indicate a lack of ac-</p>
  <p>tive participation include a lack of ac-</p>
  <p>tive management and operating the activity, hav-</p>
  <p>ing only to discharge the manager of the activity, and</p>
  <p>having an owner who is actively in an in-</p>
  <p>dependent contractor role.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Partners and corporation shareholders.</h3>
  <p>Partners and shareholders may aggregate activi-</p>
  <p>ties of their partnership or corporation within</p>
  <p>the scope of the at-risk rules if:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Films and video tapes,</li>
    <li>Farm, oil and gas properties, and</li>
    <li>Geothermal properties.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For example, if a partnership or S corpora-</p>
  <p>tion has more than 10% of the value of the par-</p>
  <p>tners or S corporation shareholders may treat the production of both films or video tapes as one activity for the at-risk rules.</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 16pt;">At-Risk Amounts</h2>
  <p>You are at risk in any activity for:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The amount you have invested and adjusted basis of property to contribute to the activity, and</li>
    <li>Amounts you borrow for use in the activity if:</li>
    <ul>
      <li>A. You are personally liable for repay-</p>
      <li>ment, or</li>
      <li>B. You pledge property (other than prop-</p>
      <li>erty used in the activity) as security for the loan.</li>
    </ul>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 16pt;">Amounts Not At Risk</h2>
  <p>You are not considered at risk for amounts pro-</p>
  <p>tected against loss through nonrecourse financ-</p>
  <p>ing arrangements, or other similar arrangements, or other</p>
  <p>similar arrangements.</p>
  <p>Nonrecourse financing. Nonrecourse financ-</p>
  <p>ing is financing for which you are not personally</p>
  <p>liable. If you borrow money to contribute to an</p>
  <p>activity, you are not at risk for the amount borrowed if the</p>
  <p>activity is not a loan. The loan is nonrecourse.</p>
  <p>If you borrow money to contribute to an activity, you are not at risk for the amount borrowed if the loan is nonrecourse. The loan is nonrecourse.</p>
  <p>Certain borrowed amounts excluded. Even if you are personally liable for the repay-</p>
  <p>ment of a borrowed amount or you secure a</p>
  <p>loan by pledging property (other than prop-</p>
  <p>erty used in the activity), you are not considered at</p>
  <p>risk if you borrowed money from the person who</p>
  <p>is not a partner or shareholder of the activity, or one related to a person (other than you) having</p>
  <p>an interest in the activity. This does not apply to</p>
  <p>amounts borrowed by a corporation or a</p>
  <p>person whose only interest in the activity is</p>
  <p>as a shareholder of the corporation.</p>
  <p>Amounts borrowed by a third party. If a third</p>
  <p>party, other than you, borrows money to contribute to an</p>
  <p>activity, you are not at risk for the amount borrowed.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">Related persons.</h3>
  <p>Related persons. Related persons in-</p>
  <p>clude:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Members of a family, but only an indi-</p>
    <li>vidual's spouse, siblings, half-brothers and sisters, parents, grandparents, and etc., and lineal descend-</p>
    <li>ants (children, grandchildren, etc.).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Related persons are those persons that are members of the same controlled group of corporations de-</p>
  <p>termined by applying a 10% ownership</p>
  <p>test.</p>
  <h3 style="font-size: 14pt;">The fiduciary of two different trusts, or the fiduciary and beneficiary of two differ-</p>
  <p>ent trusts, if the same person is the grantor</p>
  <p>of both.</p>
  <p>A tax expert or educational chartable or</p>
  <p>organization and a person who directly or in-</p>
  <p>directly controls it (or a member of whose</p>
  <p>family controls it).</p>
  <p>A corporation and an individual who owns</p>
  <p>directly or indirectly more than 10% of the</p>
  <p>value of the outstanding stock of the cor-</p>
  <p>poration.</p>
  <p>A trust fiduciary and a corporation of which</p>
  <p>more than 10% of the value is owned directly or</p>
  <p>indirectly by or for the grantor of the trust.</p>
  <p>The grantor and fiduciary, or the fiduciary</p>
  <p>and beneficiary, of any trust.</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 16pt;">How To Get Tax Help</h2>
  <p>You can get help with unresolved tax issues, or</p>
  <p>get free help with your tax return. Free help is</p>
  <p>available from IRS-certified volunteers. The Volunteer In-</p>
  <p>formation Center (VIC) is a program de-</p>
  <p>signed to help taxpayers with their tax returns. The Counseling for the Elderly (TCE) is another</p>
  <p>program that provides free tax help to seniors and older with their tax returns. Most VITA and</p>
  <p>TCE sites offer free electronic filing and all volun-</p>
  <p>teers are trained to help you with your tax return. You can find a list of VITA and TCE sites at <span data-field="url">{{ url }}</span>.</p>
  <p>If you need help with a tax issue, you can call the IRS at 1-800-829-1040.</p>
